					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-3606099#post-3594912</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-3606099/grant-request-for-a-replacement-keyboard-because-the-caps-lo#post-3594912</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Sat, 16 Sep 2017 13:31:12 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Weryllium</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>2727655</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>I appreciate the response. See, I figured that since the PL format is inherently longer than the SCP format I wouldn't be able to get away with stretching the &quot;grant request for a keyboard&quot; joke long enough without wearing <em>that</em> out (you think it can stand on its own, and maybe it can but I couldn't figure out a way to do so) and thus I opted to go the &quot;wacky escalation&quot; route you see in a lot of -J's (<a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-231-j">SCP-231-J</a> comes to mind).</p> <p>However, thank you for your input, and I will definitely take this advice into consideration if I write any other joke articles in the future, which probably won't be for some time.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-3606099#post-3594891</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-3606099/grant-request-for-a-replacement-keyboard-because-the-caps-lo#post-3594891</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Sat, 16 Sep 2017 12:38:55 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>bittermixin</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>3085453</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>This has garnered a <em>lot</em> of positive reception, so I'm going against the grain a little.</p> <p>I don't find this funny. At all, really.</p> <p>It very much overstayed its welcome and didn't feel as though it fully took advantage of its own concept. You have the premise of a grant request for a broken keyboard, and I think with the proper execution that idea alone is funny enough to support its own weight. The introduction of this weird 'hivemind' towards the end felt shoehorned, and broke the charming simplicity of the original joke.</p> <p>I don't expect you to change the article now, of course, because it's obviously hit its mark and done very well for itself, but I wanted to offer something to bear in mind for your future -J articles. Sometimes less is more, you know? -1</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
